.TH FAAD 1 "2026-10-02" "3.0.0" "Freeware Advanced Audio Decoder"

.SH NAME
faad \- open source MPEG-4 and MPEG-2 AAC decoder

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B faad
.RI [ options ]
.RI [\-o\  outfile ]
.I infile
.PP
.RI < infile >
is an ADTS
.B .aac
stream or an MP4
.B .m4a
file.

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B FAAD
is an open source MPEG-4 and MPEG-2 AAC decoder, it is licensed under the LGPL license.
It decodes AAC-LC and HE-AAC v1 (SBR) streams, and HE-AAC v2 (PS) when built with
parametric stereo support, to PCM.
It reads ADTS or MP4/M4A/M4B and writes WAV or raw PCM.
.PP
By default the output is a WAV file named after the input, with the extension
replaced by
.BR .wav .
Input from stdin ("\-") is written to stdout instead.
Decoding progress updates and summary messages are printed to stderr unless
.B -v0
or
.B -q
is given; when piping PCM to stdout ("\-w" or "\-o \-"), progress updates are automatically suppressed.

.SH I/O AND FORMAT OPTIONS
ADTS and MP4 input support files larger than 4 GiB.
ADTS input is decoded incrementally; MP4 input from a pipe is spooled to a
temporary file before decoding.
.TP
.BR -o ,\  --output\ <\fIfile\fP>
Set output filename.
An existing file is not overwritten unless
.B --overwrite
is given.
.TP
.BR -w ,\  --stdout
Write the output to stdout.
A WAV header written to stdout declares an unknown length, so readers
consume it until end of file.
.TP
.BR -f ,\  --format\ <\fItype\fP>
Output container format: wav (default) or raw (headerless PCM).
Any other value is an error.
Seekable WAV output automatically uses RF64 when its RIFF size exceeds
the 32-bit limit.
.TP
.BR -b ,\  --bits\ <\fIdepth\fP>
Sample depth: 16 (default), 24, 32 (24-bit integer in 32-bit samples) or
32f (32-bit float).
Any other value is an error.
More than two channels are written as WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE with a channel mask.
.TP
.BR -a ,\  --adts\ <\fIfile\fP>
Extract the raw ADTS stream from an MP4 file without decoding.
Requires MP4 input.

.SH PROCESSING OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -d ,\  --downmix\ [\fImode\fP]
Downmix audio to mono (mono or 1, the default) or stereo (stereo or 2).
The mode may follow as a separate argument, as in
.BR "-d stereo" ,
or be attached, as in
.B -dstereo
or
.BR --downmix=2 .
Any other value is taken as the input file name.
Has no effect on mono or stereo input.
.TP
.BR -j ,\  --jump\ <\fIseconds\fP>
Start decoding from the specified timestamp, in seconds.
Only applies to MP4 input.
A timestamp past the end gives an empty output.
.TP
.BR -g ,\  --no-gapless
Disable the gapless trim of encoder delay and trailing padding that is
otherwise applied to MP4 input.
The output then keeps the extra priming and padding samples.

.TP
.BR --drc-cut\ <\fIN\fP> ,\  --drc-boost\ <\fIN\fP>
Dynamic range control strength, 0 through 127; the default 0 is off.
.B --drc-cut
attenuates loud passages and
.B --drc-boost
raises quiet ones, using the gain control the stream carries.
Streams without DRC data are unaffected.
Boosted integer output is soft-limited near full scale; float output is not.
.TP
.BR --drc-target\ <\fIN\fP>
Normalise loudness to a target level: 0 is off, 40 through 127 is the level in
quarter-dB below full scale (96 is \-24 dBFS).
Needs a stream that carries a programme reference level.

.SH INFORMATION AND GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -i ,\  --info
Display bitstream and container metadata, then exit.
Works on ADTS and MP4 input.
.TP
.BR --json
Decode the audio frames and output stream and container information in JSON
format.
.TP
.BR --track\ <\fIid\fP>
MP4 input: decode the AAC track with this track ID.
The default is the first AAC track.
.B -i
lists the tracks of the file.
.TP
.BI -v\  verbose
Verbosity level;
.B -v
accepts values from 0 through 2;
.B -v0
is quiet mode and suppresses decoding progress;
.B -v1
(default) shows decoding progress;
.B -v2
shows decoding progress and a detailed summary block.
.TP
.BR -q ,\  --quiet
Same as
.BR -v0 .
.TP
.BR --overwrite
Overwrite an existing output file.
.TP
.BR --strict
Strict mode, report the file offset, frame number and error of a decoding
failure and exit with status 1.
Concealed core audio and recovered audio with damaged SBR also count as failures.
Without it, usable concealed or recovered audio is kept, bad packets are skipped
where framing permits, and decoding continues.
.TP
.BR --license
Display copyright and license information.
.TP
.BR -h ,\  --help
Display a short help text.
.BR --help-io ,
.B --help-processing
and
.B --help-info
show the options of one group.

.SH EXAMPLES
Decode to
.BR song.wav :
.IP
.B faad song.m4a
.PP
Decode to 24-bit WAV:
.IP
.B faad -b 24 -o out.wav song.m4a
.PP
Stream raw 16-bit PCM to another program:
.IP
.B faad -w -f raw song.m4a | aplay -f cd
.PP
Downmix surround to stereo:
.IP
.B faad -d stereo -o out.wav surround.m4a
.PP
Print stream information as JSON:
.IP
.B faad --json song.m4a
.PP
Check a file for damage:
.IP
.B faad --strict --overwrite -o /dev/null song.m4a

.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success and
.B 1
on a usage or option error, an unreadable input, an unwritable or already existing output, or, with
.BR --strict ,
a decoding error.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR faac (1),
.BR faam (1),
.BR faar (1)
.PP
.BR faac (1)
is the AAC encoder.
It is built and installed unless the
.B encoder
build option is disabled.
.PP
.BR faam (1)
is the MP4 muxer and tag tool.
It is built and installed unless the
.B muxer
build option is disabled.
